est', 'Tigre', 'Tigres', 'Tijuana', 'Tolima', 'Toluca', 'Trujillanos (VEN)', 'Universidad Católica', 'Universidad de Chile', 'Universidad de Concepción', 'Universitario (PER)', 'Universitario de Sucre', 'Universitario-PER', 'Unión Española', 'Unión La Calera', 'Vasco', 'Vélez Sarsfield', 'Zamora', 'Zulia']\n","metadata":{"output":"['Alianza Lima', 'Always Ready', 'América de Cali', 'América-MG', 'Argentinos Juniors', 'Arsenal Sarandí', 'Athletico', 'Atlas', 'Atlético Nacional', 'Atlético Tucumán', 'Atlético-MG', 'Atlético-PR', 'Barcelona-EQU', 'Binacional', 'Boca Juniors', 'Bolívar', 'Botafogo', 'Caracas', 'Cerro Porteño', 'Chapecoense', 'Cobresal', 'Colo-Colo', 'Colón', 'Corinthians', 'Cruzeiro', 'Danubio', 'Defensa y Justicia', 'Defensor Sporting', 'Delfín', 'Delfín-EQU', 'Deportes Iquique', 'Deportes Tolima', 'Deportivo Anzoátegui', 'Deportivo Cali', 'Deportivo Lara', 'Deportivo Táchira', 'Emelec', 'Estudiantes', 'Estudiantes de Mérida', 'Flamengo', 'Fluminense', 'Fortaleza', 'Godoy Cruz', 'Grêmio', 'Guaraní (PAR)', 'Guaraní-PAR', 'Huachipato', 'Huracán', 'Ind. Santa Fe', 'Independiente', 'Independiente Del Valle', 'Independiente Medellín', 'Independiente Petrolero', 'Independiente del Valle', 'Internacional', 'Jorge Wilstermann', 'Juan Aurich', 'Junior de Barranquilla', 'LDU', 'La Guaira', 'Lanús', 'León', 'Libertad', 'Libertad-PAR', 'Melgar', 'Millonarios', 'Mineros de Guaiana', 'Monagas', 'Montevideo Wanderers', 'Nacional (PAR)', 'Nacional (URU)', 'Nacional-URU', 'Newells Old Boys', \"O'Higgins\", 'Olimpia-PAR', 'Palestino', 'Palmeiras', 'Peñarol', 'Pumas', 'Racing Club', 'Real Atlético', 'Real Garcilaso', 'Red Bull Bragantino', 'Rentistas ', 'River Plate', 'River Plate-URU', 'Rosario Central', 'San José', 'San Lorenzo', 'Santos', 'Santos Laguna', 'Sport Boys', 'Sporting Cristal', 'São Paulo', 'Talleres', 'The Strongest', 'Tigre', 'Tigres', 'Tijuana', 'Tolima', 'Toluca', 'Trujillanos (VEN)', 'Universidad Católica', 'Universidad de Chile', 'Universidad de Concepción', 'Universitario (PER)', 'Universitario de Sucre', 'Universitario-PER', 'Unión Española', 'Unión La Calera', 'Vasco', 'Vélez Sarsfield', 'Zamora', 'Zulia']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nteams = sorted(set(r['home_team'] for r in rows) | set(r['away_team'] for r in rows))\nprint(teams)\n\"","time":{"start":1788243969984,"end":1788243969986}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The MCP 2.x SDK has `MCPServer` with `@server.tool()` decorator, similar to FastMCP. Good.\n\nNow let me plan the full implementation:\n\n**Project structure:**\n```\nbrazilian_soccer/\n    __init__.py\n    models.py       — Match, Player, TeamStats dataclasses\n    normalize.py    — team name normalization, date parsing\n    loader.py       — load all 6 CSVs into unified datasets\n    stats.py        — statistics engine (team stats, h2h, standings, aggregates)\n    server.py       — MCP server with tools\ntests/\n    test_match_queries.py  — BDD GWT tests\n    test_team_queries.py\n    test_player_queries.py\n    test_competition_queries.py\n    test_statistics.py\n    test_mcp_server.py     — MCP protocol tests (call tools via server.call_tool)\nserver.py (entry point, top-level) or brazilian-soccer-mcp.py\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md (update)\n```\n\nActually, TASK.md says \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" Hmm — \"Write all code files to the current directory.\" That suggests files in the current directory, not necessarily subdirectories. But a well-organized project would use packages. The instruction \"Write all code files to the current directory\" likely means don't write elsewhere (e.g., don't scatter files in random places). To be safe and reasonably organized, I'll keep it flat-ish: a package `brazilian_soccer/` plus top-level `server.py` entry point and `tests/` directory. Hmm, but strictly \"all code files to the current directory\" — I think a package directory inside the current directory is fine and standard. But to be extra literal, I could put everything flat in the current directory:\n\n- `models.py`\n- `normalize.py`\n- `loader.py`\n- `stats.py`\n- `mcp_server.py`\n- `tests/` — hmm tests need to be somewhere. `test_*.py` files can also be in the current directory, and pytest will pick them up.\n\nLet me go flat: all .py files in the current directory. That satisfies \"write all code files to the current directory\" most literally. Tests: `test_match_queries.py`, `test_team_queries.py`, etc. in the current directory. pytest will discover them. The task also says \"Maintain a detailed context block comment at the start of every code file\" — wait no, that's prompts.txt which says to ignore it. TASK.md doesn't ask for that. But it doesn't hurt to have module docstrings. I'll add good docstrings (not block comments per se — docstrings are more Pythonic). Actually the CLAUDE.md says \"DO NOT ADD ANY COMMENTS unless asked\" — but module docstrings are fine for documentation. TASK.md itself doesn't ask for comments. I'll use docstrings only, minimal inline comments.\n\nWait — the system prompt says \"IMPORTANT: DO NOT ADD ***ANY*** COMMENTS unless asked\". The user's TASK says nothing about comments. So no inline comments. Docstrings are documentation, not comments — they're fine and good practice, and important for MCP tool descriptions. I'll keep inline comments to zero and use docstrings.\n\n**Key design:**\n\n1. **normalize.py**:\n   - `strip_accents(text)` — unicodedata NFD, remove combining marks\n   - `normalize_team_name(name)` — lowercase, strip accents, remove state suffix (`-sp`, ` - rj`, `(uru)`, `antigo ...` parenthetical), collapse whitespace. Also map known aliases via a canonical mapping table (e.g., \"atletico mineiro\" -> canonical key \"atletico-mg\"? Hmm).\n   \n   Actually for matching, the normalized key is what matters: e.g. \"Palmeiras-SP\" -> \"palmeiras\"; \"Palmeiras\" -> \"palmeiras\"; \"Atlético Mineiro\" (FIFA) -> \"atletico mineiro\"; \"Atletico-MG\" (Brasileirao) -> \"atletico\". Uh oh — \"Atletico-MG\" would normalize to \"atletico\", and \"Atlético Mineiro\" to \"atletico mineiro\". These are the same club but normalize differently!\n\n   So I need an alias table mapping many variants to a canonical key. Let me design:\n   - After basic normalization (lowercase, strip accents, remove state suffix and country parens), look up in `ALIASES` dict to map to canonical key.\n   - Aliases needed (from the datasets):\n     - \"atletico\" + MG context: \"Atletico-MG\" -> normalized \"atletico\"; \"Atlético Mineiro\" -> \"atletico mineiro\". Both should map to canonical \"atletico-mg\" (display \"Atlético-MG\"? or \"Atlético Mineiro\").\n     - \"Atletico-PR\" -> \"atletico\"; \"Athletico-PR\" -> \"athletico\"; \"Athletico Paranaense\" -> \"athletico paranaense\"; \"Atlético Paranaense\" -> \"atletico paranaense\"; \"Coritiba PR\" vs \"Coritiba\"... The problem: \"atletico\" alone is ambiguous (Atletico-MG, Atletico-PR, Atletico-GO, Atletico-AC...). When the state suffix is stripped, ambiguity is lost. \n\n   Better approach: DON'T strip the state suffix blindly — instead, keep state info as part of the team record but normalize the *base name + state* when available. For matching a user query \"Atletico-MG\" or \"Atlético Mineiro\", map through an alias table to a canonical \"club key\".\n\n   Approach:\n   - Each match row provides team name; I compute a canonical club key:\n     - For names with explicit state suffix: `f\"{base}-{state}\"` e.g. \"atletico-mg\".\n     - For names without suffix: alias lookup, e.g. \"atletico mineiro\" -> \"atletico-mg\" (via alias table), \"flamengo\" -> \"flamengo-rj\"? Hmm, but BR-Football-Dataset has \"Flamengo\" without state.\n   \n   Simpler robust approach: canonical key = normalized base name only (state stripped), EXCEPT when the base name alone is ambiguous. Ambiguous base names in Brazilian football: \"atletico\" (MG/PR/GO/AC...), \"america\" (MG/RN/CE...), \"nacional\" (multiple), \"sport\"? Actually let me enumerate: in the datasets, teams sharing the same base name across different states:\n   - Atletico-MG, Atletico-PR, Atletico-GO (also Atletico-AC in Copa do Brasil: \"Atlético - AC\")\n   - America-MG, America-RN (\"América - RN\", \"América de Natal - RN\")\n   - Nacional (URU), Nacional (PAR) — international clubs in Libertadores\n   - Botafogo-RJ, Botafogo-PB? (\"Botafogo - PB\" maybe in Copa do Brasil)\n   - Vitoria-BA, Vitória-ES? \n   - Internacional vs Internacional-SC? Hmm.\n   - Rio Branco (multiple states in Copa do Brasil)\n\n   So: canonical key should INCLUDE the state when the state is known and the base is potentially ambiguous. Simplest consistent scheme:\n   - key = normalized(base + \"-\" + state) when state is known: \"atletico-mg\", \"flamengo-rj\", \"palmeiras-sp\".\n   - When state is unknown (BR-Football \"Flamengo\", Libertadores \"Flamengo\", FIFA \"Grêmio\"), use alias table to map base name to full key: \"flamengo\" -> \"flamengo-rj\", \"gremio\" -> \"gremio-rs\", \"atletico mineiro\" -> \"atletico-mg\", \"atletico paranaense\"/\"athletico paranaense\" -> \"atletico-pr\"/\"athletico-pr\"... hmm \"Athletico-PR\" vs \"Atletico-PR\" are the same club (rebranded 2019 but same entity). Normalize both \"athletico\" and \"atletico\" base for PR? For matching purposes I'd map \"athletico pr\"/\"athletico paranaense\"/\"atletico paranaense\" -> \"atletico-pr\".\n\n   And when a query comes in like \"Atletico\" alone (ambiguous), we can return all matches OR pick best disambiguation. For head-to-head and stats tools, if ambiguous, we return a list of candidates for the LLM to disambiguate. That's good MCP design: tools should tell the LLM what's ambiguous.\n\n   For simplicity and determinism:\n   - `canonical_team_key(raw_name, state_hint=None)`: \n     1. Clean: strip accents, lowercase, remove parenthetical country/state \"(URU)\", \"(antigo ...)\".\n     2. Detect trailing state suffix pattern ` - XX` or `-XX` where XX is 2 uppercase letters (Brazilian UF) — keep base and uf separately.\n     3. base = cleaned name without uf; uf = detected or None.\n     4. Look up base in BASE_ALIAS table: maps many base names to (canonical_base, default_uf). E.g. \"atletico mineiro\" -> (\"atletico\", \"mg\"); \"athletico paranaense\" -> (\"atletico\", \"pr\"); \"flamengo\" -> (\"flamengo\", \"rj\"); \"coritiba pr\" hmm that has \"pr\" as separate word.\n     5. Final key = canonical_base + (uf or default_uf or \"\"). If uf known use it; elif default_uf use it; else base only (international clubs like \"boca juniors\", \"river plate\").\n   \n   - Ambiguity: if final key has no UF and base is ambiguous in dataset... for Libertadores international teams, \"nacional\" appears with \"(URU)\" and \"(PAR)\" — the parenthetical IS the disambiguator. So instead of removing it entirely, use it: \"Nacional (URU)\" -> \"nacional-uru\"? Hmm. Let me instead keep country codes: \"(URU)\" -> suffix \"-uru\"? Or keep base as \"nacional uru\". Hmm.\n\n   Let me simplify: parenthetical country \"(URU)\" becomes part of the key: \"nacional-uru\", \"nacional-par\", \"barcelona-equ\" (from \"Barcelona-EQU\" — hmm that's suffix -EQU 3 letters). \"Olimpia-PAR\", \"Guaraní (PAR)\".\n\n   Rules for suffix: trailing `-(2..3 uppercase letters)` = country code (EQU, URU, PAR, ARG...). ` - XX` or `-XX` with 2 letters = Brazilian UF OR could be country (PAR is 3, so 2-letter is UF). But careful: \"4 de Julho - PI\" -> base \"4 de julho\", uf \"pi\". Good. \"A.b.c. - RN\" -> base \"a.b.c.\" -> normalize punctuation -> \"abc\". \"ABC - RN\" -> \"abc\". Both map to \"abc-rn\". \n\n   Known UFs: set of 27 Brazilian state abbreviations: AC, AL, AM, AP, BA, CE, DF, ES, GO, MA, MG, MS, MT, PA, PB, PE, PI, PR, RJ, RN, RO, RR, RS, SC, SE, SP, TO.\n\n   Country codes seen: URU, PAR, EQU, ARG, CHI, COL, VEN, PER, BOL, MEX? Let me check the Libertadores team names fully.\n\n   For FIFA clubs (Brazilian): \"Grêmio\" -> \"gremio\" -> alias -> \"gremio-rs\". \"Atlético Mineiro\" -> \"atletico mineiro\" -> alias \"atletico-mg\". \"Atlético Paranaense\" -> \"atletico-pr\". \"Sport Club do Recife\" -> \"sport-pe\". \"Ceará Sporting Club\" -> \"ceara-ce\". \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" — parenthetical \"(Minas Gerais)\" is not a country; strip parentheticals like that? Then \"america fc\" -> alias \"america-mg\". \"São Paulo\" -> \"sao paulo\" -> \"sao-paulo-sp\". \"Vasco da Gama\" -> \"vasco-rj\". \"Botafogo\" -> \"botafogo-rj\"? But FIFA \"Botafogo\" is Botafogo-RJ, yes. \"Santos\" -> \"santos-sp\". \"Bahia\" -> \"bahia-ba\". \"Vitória\" -> \"vitoria-ba\". \"Internacional\" -> \"internacional-rs\". \"Sport\" -> alias \"sport do recife\"/\"sport club do recife\" -> \"sport-pe\". \"Corinthians\" -> \"corinthians-sp\". \"Palmeiras\" -> \"palmeiras-sp\". \"Fluminense\" -> \"fluminense-rj\". \"Cruzeiro\" -> \"cruzeiro-mg\". \"Flamengo\" -> \"flamengo-rj\". \"Athletico Paranaense\" (FIFA 19 has both?) -> \"atletico-pr\". \"Goiás\" -> \"goias-go\". \"Ceará Sporting Club\" -> \"ceara-ce\". \"Chapecoense\" -> \"chapecoense-sc\". \"Paraná\" -> \"parana-pr\". \"Avaí\" -> \"avai-sc\". \"Fortaleza\"? \"EC Vitória\"? Let me enumerate FIFA Brazilian clubs and build the alias table accordingly.\n\n   Also BR-Football-Dataset team names (no accents): \"Sao Paulo\", \"Atletico Mineiro\", \"Atletico Paranaense\", \"Coritiba PR\", \"Gremio RS\", \"Avai\", \"Criciuma\", etc. \"Coritiba PR\" -> base \"coritiba pr\" -> hmm, \"pr\" as trailing word = UF. So rule: also detect trailing standalone UF word: \"coritiba pr\" -> (\"coritiba\", \"pr\"). \"Gremio RS\" -> (\"gremio\", \"rs\"). \n\n   And \"Mitre\"? no. OK.\n\n2. **models.py**: dataclasses:\n   - `Match`: date (date), home_team (display), away_team, home_goals, away_goals, competition, season, stage/round, home_key, away_key, source_file, stadium?, plus optional stats (corners etc. from BR-Football).\n   - `Player`: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, position, jersey, height, weight, preferred foot, value, wage, skill dict.\n   - `TeamRecord` / stats computed on the fly.\n\n3. **loader.py**: `SoccerData` class — loads all CSVs lazily/at init, builds indexes: matches by team key, matches by (competition, season), players by name, players by club key, etc. Competition canonical names:\n   - \"Brasileirão\" / \"Serie A\" (Brasileirao_Matches.csv) — competition \"Brasileirão Série A\"\n   - \"Copa do Brasil\" (Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv + BR-Football Copa do Brasil rows)\n   - \"Copa Libertadores\" (Libertadores_Matches.csv)\n   - BR-Football: \"Serie A\" -> also Brasileirão Série A (2014-2023, extends coverage!), \"Serie B\" -> Brasileirão Série B, \"Serie C\" -> Série C.\n   - novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv -> Brasileirão Série A (2003-2019).\n\n   Hmm wait — overlap: Brasileirao_Matches.csv covers 2012-2021; BR-Football Serie A covers 2014-2023; novo covers 2003-2019. Overlapping matches will be duplicated. Should I dedupe? For standings (\"Who won 2019 Brasileirão?\") duplicates would corrupt the calculation. Dedup key: (competition, season, date, home_key, away_key). If same match in two sources, keep the one with more info (prefer Brasileirao_Matches for round info; BR-Football for stats). Actually merging: keep first occurrence but prefer richer. Simpler: dedupe on (date, home_key, away_key, competition_family). For standings I'll use a primary source per competition to keep it deterministic:\n   - Standings for Brasileirão Série A season X: prefer Brasileirao_Matches if X in 2012-2021, else novo (2003-2011), else BR-Football (2022-2023).\n\n   Actually simpler and more robust: dedupe all matches at load time. Dedup key: (competition, season, date, home_key, away_key) — but what if two legs of Copa do Brasil same teams same date? Impossible. Same season, same date, same teams — same match. Keep the record with more detail (e.g., prefer one with round/stage). For \"search_matches\", user may pass `competition` filter; the merged match shows competition once.\n\n   But careful: BR-Football \"Serie A\" duplicates Brasileirao_Matches 2014-2021 matches — 380 per season. Deduping keeps Brasileirao ones (with round). 2022-2023 Serie A only in BR-Football. Good.\n\n   Also note: scores might disagree between sources for the same match (rare data errors) — first-wins is fine.\n\n   For per-competition stats (e.g. \"avg goals in Brasileirão\"), dedup matters too — use deduped set.\n\n   I'll keep `all_matches` deduped, and each Match records `source` files. Also keep `stats_matches` (BR-Football only) for corner/shot queries.\n\n4. **stats.py**:\n   - `team_record(matches, team_key)` -> W/D/L, GF/GA, per home/away split.\n   - `head_to_head(matches, key_a, key_b)` -> record from each perspective, list of matches.\n   - `standings(competition, season)` -> full table: pts, W/D/L, GF/GA, GD. Champion = top. Relegated = bottom 4.\n   - `biggest_wins(matches, n)`, `avg_goals`, `home_win_rate`.\n   - `derbies` — classic derby pairs: Fla-Flu (Flamengo-Fluminense), Paulista (Corinthians-Palmeiras-São Paulo-Santos), Gre-Nal (Grêmio-Internacional), Atlético-MG vs Cruzeiro (Clássico Mineiro), Ba-Vi (Bahia-Vitória), etc.\n\n5. **server.py / mcp_server.py**: MCPServer with tools:\n   - `search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit)` \n   - `get_head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition, season)`\n   - `get_team_stats(team, season, competition, venue)`\n   - `search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, limit, sort)`\n   - `get_player(name)` — details\n   - `get_standings(competition, season)` \n   - `get_competition_info(competition)` — seasons available, teams, final results\n   - `get_competition_finals(competition)` — finals/final matches\n   - `soccer_statistics(competition, season, team, metric)` — avg goals, home win rate, biggest wins, best records\n   - `list_competitions()`, `list_teams(competition, season)`\n   - `find_derby_matches(season)`? Could fold into search_matches with `derby=True`. I'll add a `get_derbies` tool.\n   - `get_dataset_overview()` — data coverage summary (helps LLM).\n\n   That's ~12 tools; good coverage of the spec's categories.\n\n   Return format: structured dict + a human-readable \"summary\" text field per spec's answer formats. MCP tools returning str is simplest; returning dict with \"summary\" + \"data\" is richer. I'll return dicts (structured_output) with a \"summary\" string built per spec formats.\n\n   Entry point: `python mcp_server.py` runs stdio server. Also support `--http`? Keep stdio (spec: MCP server connected to LLM; stdio is standard).\n\n   Name the top-level file `server.py`? There's `opencode.json` already; no conflict. I'll use `server.py` as the entry, containing the MCPServer wiring, and a `brazilian_soccer` package? No wait — flat. Files:\n   - `models.py`\n   - `normalize.py`\n   - `data_loader.py`\n   - `stats.py`\n   - `server.py`\n   - `test_*.py` files\n   - `requirements.txt`\n   - `pyproject.toml`? Not required; but nice for pytest config. I'll add `pyproject.toml` with `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` and console script entry. Actually keep it simple: pyproject.toml with pytest config.\n\n   Hmm wait, \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — flat layout works with pytest rootdir.\n\n6. **Tests (BDD GWT)**: pytest with Given/When/Then structure. Use classes named by feature/scenario and functions `test_...` with GWT comments. Also session-scoped fixture loading data once. Cover:\n   - Match queries: search by team, head-to-head, by season/competition/date range, finals.\n   - Team queries: stats, home record, best scoring team, h2h compare.\n   - Player queries: by name, Brazilians, top rated at club, forwards at club, cross-file (players at club + club matches).\n   - Competition: standings 2019 (champion Flamengo), relegated 2020, Copa do Brasil finals, Libertadores finals.\n   - Statistics: avg goals, home win rate, biggest wins, best away record.\n   - Normalization: name variants match (Palmeiras-SP vs Palmeiras vs Palmeiras), accents, state suffix, FIFA \"Atlético Mineiro\" vs \"Atletico-MG\".\n   - MCP server: list tools, call tools via `server.call_tool` async, verify protocol-level responses.\n\n   BDD GWT style: I'll structure with docstrings of the Gherkin scenario and Given/When/Then step comments.\n\nPerformance: index matches by team key. Load once via module-level singleton in data_loader (lazy). ~24k rows — fine.\n\nLet me now check all Libertadores team names and all FIFA Brazilian clubs, and Copa do Brasil name oddities, to build the alias table.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ba5879d001jS7NMcbGx1y1Zd","sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","messageID":"msg_05ba3527e001uU8twDbEC2WXXS"}}
