 \"Lagarto\" (\"lagarto\",\"se\"), \"Murici\" (\"murici\",\"al\"), \"ASA\" (\"asa\",\"al\"), \"CRB\" (\"crb\",\"al\"), \"CS Alagoano\" → (\"csa\",\"al\").\n\nThis is getting long, but it improves cross-file matching and query resolution. However — I should focus: unification matters only when a club appears in MORE THAN ONE file (or in queries). Let me take a pragmatic approach: add the aliases above (comprehensive enough), and importantly implement the dual-key dedup.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — do I need to add default UFs for clubs appearing in only one file? Benefit: `find_team(\"Vitoria EC\")` → key \"vitoria ec\"... query \"Vitória\" → canonical \"vitoria\" → alias (\"vitoria\",\"ba\") → exact ✓. Without the alias for \"vitoria ec\", searching \"Vitória\" still finds it via substring (needle \"vitoria\" in base \"vitoria ec\") ✓. So single-file clubs work fine via substring search. But cross-file matches AND standings dedup need the aliases. Vitória-BA appears in: novo (Vitória), BR-Football (Vitoria EC, Vitoria), Brasileirao (Vitoria-BA), Libertadores? So YES \"vitoria ec\" needs the alias for dedup correctness of standings.\n\nLet me now check which clubs appear in multiple files with mismatched keys after my current alias set, to build the *needed* alias list systematically. Quick script: build per-file team key sets, and for each pair of files find keys in one that are \"suffix-stripped-equal\" or fuzzy-equal to keys in another but not identical.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05bb22fe8001uUo0ZuYWHYASSn","sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","messageID":"msg_05bb10bac001KuKeFZZctEY4Sg"}}
