{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788245929525,"sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_af27a27d01564ce682d6f7a3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport asyncio, inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\napp = MCPServer('t')\n@app.tool()\ndef hello(name: str) -> dict:\n    '''Say hello.'''\n    return {'msg': f'hello {name}'}\nprint('type after decorate:', type(hello))\nprint(hello('world'))\nasync def go():\n    res = await app.call_tool('hello', {'name': 'mcp'})\n    print('call_tool result:', res)\n    tools = await app.list_tools()\n    print([t.name for t in tools])\nasyncio.run(go())\n\" 2>&1 | head -20"},"output":"type after decorate: <class 'function'>\n{'msg': 'hello world'}\ncall_tool result: meta=None content=[TextContent(type='text', text='{\\n  \"msg\": \"hello mcp\"\\n}', annotations=None, meta=None)] structured_content=None is_error=False result_type='complete'\n['hello']\n","metadata":{"output":"type after decorate: <class 'function'>\n{'msg': 'hello world'}\ncall_tool result: meta=None content=[TextContent(type='text', text='{\\n  \"msg\": \"hello mcp\"\\n}', annotations=None, meta=None)] structured_content=None is_error=False result_type='complete'\n['hello']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport asyncio, inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\napp = MCPServer('t')\n@app.tool()\ndef hello(name: str) -> dict:\n    '''Say hello.'''\n    return {'msg': f'hello {name}'}\nprint('type after decorate:', type(hello))\nprint(hello('world'))\nasync def go():\n    res = await app.call_tool('hello', {'name': 'mcp'})\n    print('call_tool result:', res)\n    tools = await app.list_tools()\n    print([t.name for t in tools])\nasyncio.run(go())\n\" 2>&1 | head -20","time":{"start":1788245929522,"end":1788245929524}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"All historically verifiable:\n- 2019 Libertadores final: Flamengo 2-1 River Plate ✓ (single match, Lima)\n- 2020 Libertadores final: Palmeiras 1-0 Santos ✓ (single match)\n- 2018 CdB final: Cruzeiro ✓; 2019: Athletico-PR ✓; 2020: Palmeiras ✓\n- 2016/2017 Libertadores finals missing? 2016: Atlético Nacional x Junior; the file starts 2013... finals list showed 15 matches; last 6 shown. 2017 Grêmio ✓ 2 legs.\n\nNow the MCP server. Tools design (using mcp 2.x MCPServer):\n\n```python\nserver = MCPServer(\n    name='brazilian-soccer',\n    instructions='...',\n)\n\n@server.tool()\nasync def search_matches(...) -> dict: ...\n```\n\nWait — do tools need to be async? MCPServer supports both sync and async functions (FastMCP did; check MCPServer v2). Let me check the docs... The `tool` decorator in v2 — the migration doc says API mostly same. I'll test with a sync function; if not supported, make them async.\n\nTools:\n1. `get_dataset_overview()` — data coverage; helps the LLM know what's available.\n2. `list_teams(competition=None, season=None, search=None)` — discovery.\n3. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, limit=25)` — core match search. `stage`: \"final\", \"semifinals\", \"group stage\", or cup round label. Returns matches + summary.\n4. `get_head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None)` — h2h record + recent matches.\n5. `get_team_stats(team, competition=None, season=None, venue=None)` — record with home/away split, matches list summary.\n6. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_age=None, limit=25, sort='overall')` — player search.\n7. `get_player_details(name)` — full player profile incl. skills.\n8. `get_club_players(club, nationality=None, position=None)` — squad listing with avg rating (cross-file: club + matches combined).\n9. `get_standings(competition='Brasileirão Série A', season)` — table + champion + relegated.\n10. `get_competition_info(competition)` — seasons, matches, finals, teams.\n11. `get_competition_finals(competition)` — all final matches.\n12. `get_statistics(competition=None, season=None, team=None, metric=...)` — hmm, split into:\n    - `get_aggregate_statistics(competition=None, season=None)` — avg goals, win rates\n    - `get_biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, team=None, limit=10)`\n    - `get_best_records(competition=None, season=None, metric='points'|'win_rate'|'goals', venue='overall', limit=10)`\n13. `get_derby_matches(derby=None, season=None, team=None)` — derbies.\n\nAlso maybe `find_team(query)` exposed as a tool (helps LLM resolve \"Atletico\" ambiguity before calling other tools). Yes — `resolve_team(query)` tool.\n\nResponse format: each tool returns dict with \"summary\" (formatted string per spec examples) + structured data. On errors/ambiguity: return {\"error\": ...} or candidates.\n\nTeam resolution behavior in tools: given `team` param:\n- find_team → if top candidate is unambiguous (only one with high match_count or exact key), use it; if multiple candidates with meaningful match counts (like atletico-mg/atletico-pr/atletico-go), return ambiguity error listing candidates. Rule: resolve via exact canonical key first; if exact key not in registry, take candidates; if candidates[0].match_count > 4 * candidates[1].match_count → use it; else ambiguous → return candidates list with counts and keys, asking to disambiguate. This is clean MCP UX.\n\nEdge: \"Flamengo\" → exact key \"flamengo-rj\" ✓ direct. \"Palmeiras-SP\" → exact ✓. \"Atletico\" → candidates atletico-mg (many), atletico-pr (many), atletico-go... ambiguous → return candidates. \"Coritiba PR\" → exact ✓.\n\nNow — the spec says queries must answer \"at least 20 sample questions\". The tools cover: last match between teams (search_matches with team+opponent sorted desc → first), score, player by name, players at club, derbies in 2023, competitions a team played, best home record, top Brazilian players, compare seasons, average goals, biggest wins, standings/champion/relegated, finals, h2h, team home record, top scorers — \"Top scorers (if inferable from data)\" — not inferable (no scorer data) → get_competition_info can note that. Good.\n\nFor \"When did Flamengo last play Corinthians?\" — search_matches returns most recent first? I'll return ascending or descending? For \"recent matches\" usefulness, sort descending (most recent first) with total count. Summary shows up to limit.\n\nMCP server file: `server.py` with `main()` → `server.run()` (stdio). Also add `if __name__ == '__main__'`.\n\nAlso expose the tool functions as plain sync Python functions (testable without MCP) and register thin wrappers — better: define functions, then `server.tool()(fn)`. Actually simplest: define with decorator @server.tool() — but then testing needs the server object. I'll build the server via `build_server()` factory and keep tool functions module-level. Hmm — decorator at import time registers onto a module-level server instance. For tests, import server module and call `server.server.call_tool(...)`? MCPServer.call_tool is async. For pure logic tests, I'll call the underlying functions; for MCP protocol tests, use await server.call_tool('name', {...}).\n\nPlan: \n- module `server.py`:\n  - `app = MCPServer(...)` created via `build_server()`\n  - tool functions defined as regular functions, registered via `@app.tool()`... but `app` must exist first. I'll create `app` at module level, then decorate. For testability, the decorated functions remain callable directly? In FastMCP v1, `@mcp.tool()` returns the original fn (registers as side effect). v2 MCPServer.tool — check: returns decorator that registers and returns fn? Let me verify quickly with inspect.\n\nLet me check how MCPServer.tool decorator behaves and how call_tool works.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05bc36c6e001tI7GJBuIjUhdCG","sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","messageID":"msg_05bc29a8c00178iRW7TIvjUQTi"}}
