{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788246005800,"sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_1bbe3bca05304b73aab43ff6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python -c \"\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nimport inspect\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.run))\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.run_stdio_async))\n\""},"output":"(self, transport: \"Literal['stdio', 'sse', 'streamable-http']\" = 'stdio', **kwargs: 'Any') -> 'None'\n(self) -> 'None'\n","metadata":{"output":"(self, transport: \"Literal['stdio', 'sse', 'streamable-http']\" = 'stdio', **kwargs: 'Any') -> 'None'\n(self) -> 'None'\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python -c \"\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nimport inspect\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.run))\nprint(inspect.signature(MCPServer.run_stdio_async))\n\"","time":{"start":1788246005763,"end":1788246005799}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The decorator preserves the function (directly callable) and `call_tool` works with JSON-serialized content. Note: `structured_content=None` — by default tools returning dicts get JSON-stringified into TextContent. That's fine for LLM consumption. (There's a `structured_output` option on the tool decorator; default None probably auto-detects. Either way works — text content is what LLMs consume.)\n\nHmm wait, structured_content=None but content has the JSON text. Let me check if there's a way to get structured output... The tool decorator has `structured_output: bool | None`. With None it probably tries to infer from return annotation. My function annotated `-> dict` returned dict but structured_content=None. Maybe needs TypedDict annotation? Not critical — text JSON is fine.\n\nNow write server.py. Tool list (13 tools):\n\n1. get_dataset_overview\n2. resolve_team(query)\n3. list_teams(competition, season, search)\n4. search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit)\n5. get_head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition, season)\n6. get_team_stats(team, competition, season, venue)\n7. search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, limit, sort)\n8. get_player_details(name)\n9. get_club_players(club, nationality, position)\n10. get_standings(competition, season)\n11. get_competition_info(competition)\n12. get_competition_finals(competition)\n13. get_aggregate_statistics(competition, season)\n14. get_biggest_wins(competition, season, team, limit)\n15. get_best_records(competition, season, metric, venue, limit)\n16. get_derby_matches(derby, team, season)\n\nThat's 16 tools. \n\nDesign details:\n\n**Team resolution helper** (module-level, shared):\n```python\ndef _resolve_team(query, data) -> tuple[Optional[str], Optional[dict]]:\n    candidates = data.find_team(query)\n    if not candidates: return None, {'error': f\"No team matching '{query}'...\"}\n    top = candidates[0]\n    if len(candidates) == 1: return top.key, None\n    second = candidates[1]\n    if top.match_count >= 5 * max(second.match_count,1): return top.key, None\n    # ambiguous\n    return None, {'error': 'ambiguous', 'candidates': [...]}\n```\nHmm — but \"Flamengo\" exact → candidates[0] = flamengo-rj (exact) + \"flamengo do piaui-pi\" + \"flamengo-pi\" (tiny, 2 matches each) → ratio huge → resolve ✓. \"Atletico\" → atletico-mg (1090), atletico-pr (900), atletico-go (400) → ambiguous ✓ good. \"Santos\" → santos-sp (2000+) vs santos laguna (players only, 0 matches... but maybe 20 players!) — match_count for laguna = 0 (players counted separately) → ratio: top 2000+ vs 0 → resolve santos-sp ✓. Hmm but is that right? \"Which players play for Santos?\" → FIFA \"Santos\" = Santos FC (SP) — correct resolution ✓. \"Santos Laguna players\" → find_team('Santos Laguna') → exact key \"santos laguna\" ✓.\n\nEdge: \"Botafogo\" → botafogo-rj (big) vs botafogo-pb, botafogo-sp — all in data. botafogo-rj ~1000, botafogo-sp ~200 (Série B 2019+?), botafogo-pb ~30 → 1000 vs 200 → 5x → resolve rj. Hmm — borderline but OK (RJ is the default meaning).\n\n\"America\" → america-mg (~500) vs america-rn (~50)? → 10x → resolve MG. Reasonable.\n\n**search_matches** filter logic:\n- resolve team → if error return\n- candidate matches = data.matches_for_team(key); if opponent: filter involves(opponent_key)\n- if competition: filter\n- if season: filter (also season might be string \"2023\" → int)\n- date range: parse date_from/date_to (accept ISO or DD/MM/YYYY)\n- stage: match stage/round case-insensitive contains (\"final\" must not match \"quarterfinal\"! Use exact match on normalized label, or special: \"final\" == \"Final\"/\"final\" exact). stage options: group stage, round of 16, quarterfinals, semifinals, final; cup rounds: Final, Semifinal, Quarterfinal, Round of 16, Round N. I'll normalize: lower + strip; compare equality OR (query 'final' matches 'final' only, not 'quarterfinals') — exact equality after lowercase. But user may say \"semifinal\" vs data \"Semifinal\" ✓ equal. \"round of 16\" ✓. Also \"quarterfinal\" vs \"Quarterfinal\" ✓. OK equality on lowercase; also accept \"quarterfinals\"↔\"quarterfinal\"? I'll map both singular/plural forms: strip trailing 's' for comparison. Let me do: norm(q) in {norm(label), norm(label).rstrip('s')}.\n\n- Sort: most recent first? I'll sort descending by date and return matches with date/score/competition/round/stage. Include total_count; \"showing first N\".\n\n**get_head_to_head**: resolve both teams → h2h over filtered matches (competition/season) → record + matches list (most recent first, limit maybe 15 in summary but full data available? I'll include up to `limit=15` matches in response).\n\n**get_team_stats**: resolve team; filter matches by competition/season; compute TeamRecord; venue param: 'all'|'home'|'away' — the record already includes home/away splits; venue filters which matches count (only home matches etc.). Return record + summary + recent matches (last 5).\n\n**search_players**: filters: name (contains), nationality (exact-ish contains, e.g. \"Brazil\"), club (resolve via find_team on club then match club_key — careful: club in FIFA might be non-Brazilian \"Paris Saint-Germain\" → find_team('Paris Saint-Germain') → canonical key \"paris saint germain\"... is it in teams registry? FIFA clubs are registered as teams (via _register_team in _load_players) ✓. But find_team substring matching over 1159 teams... \"Paris Saint-Germain\" → key exact ✓). position: FIFA code (ST, LW...) or group (forward/midfielder/defender/goalkeeper)? Support both: map friendly names to position groups. min_overall, sort by overall/potential/age/name.\n\nPosition groups:\n- GK: ['GK']\n- DF: ['CB','LCB','RCB','LB','RB','LWB','RWB']\n- MF: ['CDM','LDM','RDM','CM','LCM','RCM','CAM','LAM','RAM','LM','RM']\n- FW: ['ST','LS','RS','CF','LF','RF','LW','RW']\n\n**get_player_details(name)**: exact-ish search; return first match (or list if multiple) with full skills.\n\n**get_club_players(club)**: resolve club key → players at club; include avg overall, top players; ALSO cross-file: club's match stats (recent matches, record) — \"Cross-file queries work (e.g., player + match data)\" ✓ this is the cross-file tool.\n\n**get_standings(competition, season)**: default competition \"Brasileirão Série A\". Resolve competition alias. Get matches → standings table + champion/relegated + matches_played + note if partial. Return full table.\n\n**get_competition_info(competition)**: seasons, match count per season (or total), teams count, finals list, top scorer note (not available).\n\n**get_competition_finals(competition)**: finals list w/ winner determination for 2-leg ties (aggregate)? That's nice: for Libertadores finals pre-2019: two legs — compute aggregate winner. Let me implement: group finals by season; if 2 matches → aggregate score; winner = aggregate (or the file already... no penalties info). For single match: winner = match winner. CdB finals: 2 legs → aggregate. Implement `_finals_summary`.\n\n**get_aggregate_statistics(competition, season)**: competition_aggregates + optionally by-team? Keep simple: aggregates + per-season breakdown if no season given? I'll include: overall aggregates + per-season table when competition given without season.\n\n**get_biggest_wins(competition, season, team, limit)**.\n\n**get_best_records(competition, season, metric, venue, limit)**: venue 'home'|'away'|'all' — for home/away, compute records from only home/away matches of each team. Implementation: for venue home: matches where team is home; team_record handles. I'll filter matches per team: for 'home', use matches where home_key == team. best_records() from stats.py uses all matches per team; I'll extend: pass venue filter → modify best_records to accept venue. Let me add venue param to best_records in stats.py.\n\n**get_derby_matches(derby, team, season)**: filter by derby name (case-insensitive contains) or team or season.\n\nSummary strings: follow spec formats.\n\nTime to write server.py. Also entry point `main()` with argparse: default stdio; `--http` runs streamable HTTP on 8000? Keep stdio only per spec, but a `--transport` option is nice. MCPServer v2: `run()` — check signature: run(transport=...)? Let me check quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05bc496770014QTUDEk5wpVS7N","sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","messageID":"msg_05bc36f90001aUUosiBnJITM0I"}}
