 | brfoot: ['4 de Julho EC']\n","metadata":{"output":"Metropolitano | cup: [] | brfoot: ['Metropolitano Maringa PR']\nBrasil | cup: ['Brasil - RS', 'Brasiliense - DF', 'Red Bull Brasil - SP'] | brfoot: ['Brasil de Pelotas', 'Brasilia FC', 'Brasiliense', 'Red Bull Brasil']\nAltos | cup: ['Altos - PI'] | brfoot: ['AE Altos', 'Altos']\nGlobo | cup: ['Globo - RN'] | brfoot: ['Globo FC']\nGenus | cup: ['Genus - RO'] | brfoot: ['SC Genus']\nOperário | cup: ['Operário - MS', 'Operário - MT', 'Operário - PR'] | brfoot: []\nOperario | cup: ['Novoperario - MS', 'Operario - PR', 'Operario Ferroviario Esporte C - PR'] | brfoot: ['Operario FC MS', 'Operario MS', 'Operario MT', 'Operario PR']\nSerra | cup: ['Serra F. C. - ES'] | brfoot: ['Serra']\nBoavista | cup: ['Boavista - RJ', 'Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ'] | brfoot: ['Boavista', 'Boavista RJ', 'Boavista SC Saquarema']\nCampinense | cup: ['Campinense - PB'] | brfoot: ['Campinense', 'Campinense Clube']\nRetro | cup: [] | brfoot: ['Retro', 'Retro FC Brasil']\n4 de Julho | cup: ['4 de Julho - PI'] | brfoot: ['4 de Julho EC']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\nfrom collections import defaultdict\ncup = set()\nwith open('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n    for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n        cup.add(r['home_team']); cup.add(r['away_team'])\nbf = set()\nwith open('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv', encoding='utf-8-sig') as f:\n    for r in csv.DictReader(f):\n        bf.add(r['home']); bf.add(r['away'])\nfor kw in ['Metropolitano','Brasil','Altos','Globo','Genus','Operário','Operario','Serra','Boavista','Campinense','Retro','4 de Julho']:\n    c = sorted(t for t in cup if kw.lower() in t.lower())\n    b = sorted(t for t in bf if kw.lower() in t.lower())\n    if c or b: print(kw, '| cup:', c[:4], '| brfoot:', b[:4])\n\"","time":{"start":1788247184320,"end":1788247184322}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Excellent — 57 genuine dedup misses, all in Copa do Brasil (cup file vs BR-Football). These are small clubs where BR-Football adds/omits suffixes and legal-type names (\"AE Altos\" vs \"Altos\", \"CE Dom Bosco\", \"SC Genus\", \"Globo FC\", \"Metropolitano Maringá\", \"Brasil de Pelotas\" vs \"Brasil\", etc.).\n\nInteresting: \"brasil-rs\" — the cup file has \"Brasil - RS\" (Brasil de Pelotas) and my alias maps \"brasil de pelotas\" → (\"brasil de pelotas\",\"rs\")... wait the pair shows 'brasil-rs' vs 'brasil de pelotas-rs' — so cup file has \"Brasil - RS\" → base \"brasil\" + uf rs → \"brasil-rs\"; BR-Football \"Brasil de Pelotas\" → alias → \"brasil de pelotas-rs\". Need alias \"brasil\" → (\"brasil de pelotas\",\"rs\")?? But \"Brasil\" alone... risky, but in this dataset \"Brasil - RS\" is always Brasil de Pelotas. Add alias \"brasil\" → (\"brasil de pelotas\", \"rs\").\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety: these pairs differ in one team key while the OTHER side matches exactly — e.g. ('2015-02-25', 'globo-rn' vs 'globo fc' both hosting flamengo-rj with same score, same date ±1). These are dedup misses for CUP matches vs BR-Football cup matches. The fixes needed (aliases):\n- \"globo fc\" → (\"globo\",\"rn\")\n- \"amadense ec\" → (\"amadense\",\"se\")\n- \"metropolitano maringa\" — cup has \"Metropolitano-PR\"? key \"metropolitano maringa-pr\"? The pair: 'maringa-pr' vs 'metropolitano maringa-pr' — cup file \"Metropolitano (Maringá) - PR\"? hmm. BR-Football has \"Maringa\" → alias (\"maringa\",\"pr\") → \"maringa-pr\"; cup file has... something that keys \"metropolitano maringa-pr\". Hmm — Metropolitano is a DIFFERENT club (SC)! Metropolitano de Maringá... hmm, actually no: the club is \"Grêmio Metropolitano Maringá\"? Hmm. In 2015 Copa do Brasil first round: \"Maringá x Madureira\"? Hmm, hmm. Actually there might be genuine ambiguity here — Metropolitano-SC is from Blumenau. But same date+score vs Madureira... the cup file probably has \"Metropolitano-SC\"? but we got \"metropolitano maringa-pr\". Let me check the cup raw. Hmm — actually wait, this could be TWO different matches: cup file \"Metropolitano (PR)\" hmm.\n\nHmm, wait: actually, let me reconsider. Row: ('2015-03-05', 'Copa do Brasil', 'maringa-pr', 'metropolitano maringa-pr', 'madureira-rj') — home side differs: 'maringa-pr' (BR-Football \"Maringa\") vs 'metropolitano maringa-pr' (cup raw probably \"Metropolitano Maringá - PR\"?? hmm). Hmm — hmm, this might be a legitimately different club name for the same club (Grêmio Metropolitano? no). The club from Maringá that played Madureira in 2015 CdB round 1... I believe it's \"Grêmio Metropolitano\"? No — hmm. Let me look at raw names in both files for these cases rather than guessing.\n\nAlso \"operario-ms\" vs \"operario fc-ms\": BR-Football \"Operario FC\"? and cup \"Operário - MS\"? Hmm wait — Operário-MS (Campo Grande) vs Operário-PR (Ponta Grossa) are different clubs! \"operario fc-ms\" vs \"operario-ms\" — same club (MS). Alias \"operario fc\" → (\"operario\",\"ms\")? But BR-Football might also have \"Operario PR\" → \"operario-pr\" ✓ distinct. OK.\n\nLet me extract the raw names for all these pairs from the files, then write a comprehensive alias fix. Rather than a huge manual alias table, consider a GENERIC approach: after loading, a second-pass \"fuzzy dedup\": for matches in the same competition with same date ±1, same score, and one team key equal + other key similar (token-subset after suffix strip) → treat as duplicate. That's data-driven and avoids enumerating every small club. But \"similar\" needs care: 'maringa-pr' vs 'metropolitano maringa-pr' — token subset ✓ (\"maringa\" ⊂ \"metropolitano maringa\"). 'brasil-rs' vs 'brasil de pelotas-rs' — subset ✓. 'globo-rn' vs 'globo fc' — base \"globo\" vs \"globo fc\" subset ✓. 'ae altos' vs 'altos-pi': base \"ae altos\" vs \"altos\" — subset ✓.\n\nRisk of over-merging: two DIFFERENT clubs with subset names playing same-side vs same opponent, same date, same score, same competition — essentially impossible.\n\nI'll implement a post-load fuzzy merge pass in the loader:\n- Build index: (comp, date_ordinal, score, away_key) → list of match indices (for home-side candidates) and (comp, date, score, home_key) → (away-side candidates).\n- For each match, look for another match with same comp, date ±1, same score, identical opposite-side key, and base-subset-similar same-side key → merge (drop the one with fewer... prefer: keep the one whose key has a UF suffix? or keep the higher-priority source — priority order = list order since loaded sequentially; keep earlier).\n\nRules for \"similar\": base(key1) != base(key2) AND (base1 in base2 or base2 in base1) AND same UF suffix if both have one. E.g. 'brasil-rs' vs 'brasil de pelotas-rs': suffixes rs == rs ✓. 'globo-rn' vs 'globo fc': globo fc has NO suffix; base \"globo\" in \"globo fc\" ✓.\n\nWait, also need: same-direction (both home or both away) — yes, using the indexes above ensures opposite side identical.\n\nThen after merging, the dropped match's team key should ALSO be aliased in the registry: update teams registry to merge variants into the kept key? The teams dict: both keys exist as separate entries. For consistency (find_team will list both), I could remap: the fuzzy pass returns alias pairs (bad_key → good_key); then update the team entries: merge raw_variants and match_count. Hmm — but the canonical keys for future matches remain as-is (e.g. BR-Football Série C has \"AE Altos\" alone — those matches keep key \"ae altos\" but the alias \"ae altos\" → \"altos-pi\" should apply to them too for consistency!).\n\nBetter approach: LEARN the aliases from the fuzzy pass, then RELOAD with those aliases added to BASE_ALIASES dynamically. Two-phase load: load once, detect alias pairs, add to BASE_ALIASES, reload. Clean! Implementation:\n1. Load SoccerData normally.\n2. Detect candidate alias pairs: dict bad_base → (good_key). For a matched pair: determine which key is \"canonical\": prefer the key WITH a UF suffix and longer match history... Actually prefer the key that comes from the HIGHER-priority source (cup file loaded before BR-Football → kept match is the earlier one; the kept match's key is canonical). The dropped one's BASE (before suffixing? no — the key base) maps: bad_key base → canonical key base+suffix. Since BASE_ALIASES maps base → (base, uf): entry BASE_ALIASES[bad_base] = (good_base, good_uf).\n   E.g. kept key \"altos-pi\" (base \"altos\", uf pi), dropped key \"ae altos\" (base \"ae altos\") → BASE_ALIASES[\"ae altos\"] = (\"altos\", \"pi\") ✓.\n   \"brasil-rs\" kept?? Wait which is kept — the cup file loads BEFORE BR-Football, so cup's key \"brasil-rs\" is kept and BR-Football's \"brasil de pelotas-rs\" dropped → BASE_ALIASES[\"brasil de pelotas\"] would map to (\"brasil\",\"rs\")?! But my static alias says \"brasil de pelotas\" → (\"brasil de pelotas\",\"rs\"). CONFLICT — and semantically \"Brasil de Pelotas\" is the better canonical name. Hmm. For canonical choice, prefer the LONGER/more descriptive base as canonical: map the SHORTER base to the longer key. E.g. \"brasil\" → (\"brasil de pelotas\",\"rs\")?? But \"brasil\" as a base could collide elsewhere... it only appears as \"Brasil - RS\" in cup. And \"altos\" → (\"ae altos\"?) no wait — prefer longer base as canonical: \"ae altos\" is longer than \"altos\"... but \"Altos-PI\" is the proper name. Ugh.\n\n   Simplicity wins: canonical = the key of the KEPT match (higher-priority source = the dedicated cup file). For \"brasil-rs\" vs \"brasil de pelotas-rs\": kept = cup file's \"brasil-rs\" → alias \"brasil de pelotas\" → (\"brasil\",\"rs\"). This CHANGES my static alias for \"brasil de pelotas\" — I should REMOVE the static one and let the learned one win? Conflict: static aliases load first; learned aliases must not fight them. Since \"brasil de pelotas\" static → (\"brasil de pelotas\",\"rs\") produces key \"brasil de pelotas-rs\", and learned wants \"brasil-rs\"... I should just REMOVE the static \"brasil de pelotas\" alias and let the dynamic learning handle it. But display name: \"brasil-rs\" → team_display_name → \"Brasil (RS)\" — suboptimal but fine; add DISPLAY_NAMES[\"brasil-rs\"] = \"Brasil de Pelotas\".\n\n   Simpler alternative: hardcode all 57 pairs as static aliases? They're enumerable but tedious and brittle. The dynamic approach is elegant and data-driven. Let me do dynamic with rules:\n   - learned_aliases[bad_base] = (good_base, good_uf) where kept key = good.\n   - Only learn when bad_base not already in BASE_ALIASES (avoid fighting statics).\n   - After learning, add to BASE_ALIASES (module-level? no — pass into a fresh SoccerData construction). BASE_ALIASES is module-level dict — mutate it then construct new SoccerData. get_data() would do: data = SoccerData(); aliases = data._detect_alias_pairs(); BASE_ALIASES.update(aliases); data = SoccerData() (fresh reload with new aliases). Load takes 0.85s, twice = 1.7s — acceptable (< 2s simple lookup budget? The first tool call pays 1.7s. Hmm, borderline. Loading is lazy at first tool call. 1.7s < 2s OK).\n\n   Also need DISPLAY_NAMES for the learned canonical keys? team_display_name falls back to \"Base (UF)\" — fine for small clubs.\n\n   For the maringa case: kept = cup \"metropolitano maringa-pr\"?? wait — the pair was (m1='maringa-pr' [BR-Football], m2='metropolitano maringa-pr' [cup]). Cup loads first → kept = \"metropolitano maringa-pr\", learned alias: \"maringa\" → (\"metropolitano maringa\",\"pr\"). But WAIT — my static alias \"maringa\" → (\"maringa\",\"pr\") exists! Conflict → static wins → BR-Football \"Maringa\" stays \"maringa-pr\" and the dup persists. Hmm. I should REMOVE conflicting statics (\"maringa\", \"brasil de pelotas\") and let learning handle them... but only if they're actually involved in learned pairs. Let me make the learning able to OVERRIDE static aliases when they demonstrably conflict? No — simpler: if the static alias produces a DIFFERENT key than the learned canonical for a base, the static wins and the dup remains. To handle these few, remove statics: \"brasil de pelotas\", \"maringa\". Then learned: \"brasil\" → (\"brasil\",\"rs\") is identity (base brasil, uf rs) — wait kept key is \"brasil-rs\" = base \"brasil\" + uf \"rs\". Learned alias for bad base \"brasil de pelotas\": (\"brasil\",\"rs\") ✓. And \"maringa\" → (\"metropolitano maringa\",\"pr\") ✓.\n\n   Hmm wait, one more check on the maringa case — is \"Metropolitano (Maringá)\" really the same club as BR-Football's \"Maringa\"? Same date ±1, same score, opponent Madureira exactly, Copa do Brasil → yes, same fixture. The 2015 CdB first-round match Madureira x Maringá... hmm, whatever the source says — they agree on the fixture.\n\n3. The fuzzy detection: iterate over ALL matches pairwise via indexes (efficient). Compare same-direction sides: home index by (comp, date_ordinal±1, score, away_key). For candidate matches m1, m2 (m1 index < m2 index): if home keys differ but similar and away keys equal → learn alias (base(m2.home_key) → components of m1.home_key) and DROP m2? Or keep both until reload? Since we reload anyway with new aliases, the reload dedups properly. So phase 1: just LEARN aliases (don't mutate matches). Phase 2: reload with aliases → dedup happens naturally via keys. \n\nSimilarity rule:\n```python\ndef _base_of(key): return key.rsplit('-', 1)[0] if '-' in key else key\ndef _suffix_of(key): return key.rsplit('-', 1)[1] if '-' in key else None\nsimilar = b1 != b2 and (b1 in b2 or b2 in b1) and (s1 == s2 or s1 is None or s2 is None)\n```\nHmm — suffix condition: 'globo-rn' vs 'globo fc' → s1='rn', s2=None → OK (suffixless likely means the alias default will supply). 'brasil-rs' vs 'brasil de pelotas-rs' → s1==s2='rs' ✓. What about 'novo hamburgo' vs 'novo hamburgo-rs'? base same → handled by... base equal case: base1 == base2 but keys differ (one has suffix, one not): \"novo hamburgo\" vs \"novo hamburgo-rs\" — base \"novo hamburgo\" == \"novo hamburgo\", suffix None vs rs. Similar ✓ (b1 in b2 trivially). But my earlier detector required base inequality — for learning, base-equal + one-suffixless is ALSO an alias case (\"novo hamburgo\" → (\"novo hamburgo\",\"rs\")). Include: (b1 == b2 and (s1 is None) != (s2 is None)) or (b1 != b2 and subset and suffixes compatible).\n\nFor base-equal case, alias: bad = suffixless base → (base, suffix from the other).\n\nEdge: 'abc' vs 'abc-rn' — my static alias \"abc\" → (\"abc\",\"rn\") already handles ✓.\n\nAlso the earlier noise (atletico-mg vs atletico-pr, same date, same score, opposite equal) — e.g. 2012-07-29: sport-pe vs atletigo-go <> fluminense-rj vs atletico-mg — opposite sides NOT equal (sport-pe vs fluminense-rj differ) → the new detector requires opposite EQUAL, so these are excluded ✓. But what about: m1 = X vs atletico-mg, m2 = X vs atletico-pr, same date, same score? base \"atletico\" == \"atletico\" with different suffixes (mg vs pr) → my rule: b1==b2 but BOTH have suffixes and s1 != s2 → NOT similar ✓ good (they're different clubs).\n\nDanger case: same club name in different states genuinely different clubs, one suffixless... e.g. \"nacional-am\" vs \"nacional\"? If BR-Football \"Nacional\" means Nacional-AM and cup \"Nacional - AM\"... hmm, but also \"nacional-rn\"? In our data: copa has \"Nacional - AM\", \"Nacional - PB\"? Let me just run the detector and review the learned aliases before applying — with careful conditions. The suffix-compat rule (one None) protects only when bases are equal; when bases differ (subset), suffixes must be EQUAL or one None.\n\nLet me also handle direction: away-side similar + home equal, and home-similar + away equal.\n\nOne more danger: a \"similar\" pair that's actually two DIFFERENT matches (e.g., Flamengo played Copa do Brasil same-day different opponent... no — opposite side must be IDENTICAL). E.g., m1: \"gremio-rs vs flamengo-rj\", m2: \"gremio novorizontino? no — subset check \"gremio\" in \"gremio novorizontino\" ✓!! m1 home \"gremio-rs\" (cup), m2 home \"gremio novorizontino\"?? both vs same opponent same date same score in same competition — Grêmio and Grêmio Novorizontino can't both host the same team in the same competition on the same date with the same score. Actually possible in theory (coincidence), astronomically unlikely. Accept.\n\nBut WAIT — one real risk: \"sport\" vs \"sport boys\"! Libertadores: \"Sport Boys\" (Bolivia) and \"Sport\" (Recife). If both hosted the same opponent (e.g. Palmeiras) in the same competition ±1 day with the same score... different seasons though! Add season to the match key: same season required. Sport Boys 2014/2017 Libertadores, Sport-PE 2020/2021 Libertadores — no overlap ✓. Add season to index key for safety.\n\nAlso \"atletico\" family: \"atletico-mg\" vs \"atletico goianiense\"? bases \"atletico\" vs \"atletico goianiense\" — subset ✓, suffixes mg vs go — DIFFERENT → rejected ✓ (my static alias already maps \"atletico goianiense\" → (\"atletico\",\"go\")... hmm wait, that alias remaps \"atletico goianiense\" base to \"atletico\" + uf go → key \"atletico-go\" ✓ good).\n\n\"atletico nacional\" vs \"atletico-mg\": bases \"atletico nacional\" vs \"atletico\" — subset! suffixes: \"atletico nacional\" no suffix, \"atletico-mg\" suffix mg → one None → SIMILAR → would learn \"atletico nacional\" → (\"atletico\",\"mg\")!!! DISASTER — Atlético Nacional (Colombia) is NOT Atlético Mineiro! When could this trigger? Both host same opponent, same comp, same season, same date ±1, same score: \"Atletico Nacional vs X\" and \"Atletico-MG vs X\" in the same competition+season — Libertadores 2014: Atletico-MG played Atletico Nacional! If BOTH hosted e.g. \"Nacional-URU\"... hmm. Rather than reason through every case, require the SUFFIXES to be EQUAL when both exist, and when one is None... allow only if the base-without-suffix EQUALS (not subset). Revised rule:\n- b1 == b2 and (s1 is None) != (s2 is None) → alias (the suffixless one gets the suffix)\n- b1 != b2, subset, and s1 == s2 (both None or both same) → alias\n\n\"atletico nacional\" (b1, s=None) vs \"atletico-mg\" (b2='atletico', s='mg'): b1 != b2, s1=None ≠ s2='mg' → REJECTED ✓.\n\"globo fc\" (b='globo fc', s=None) vs \"globo-rn\" (b='globo', s='rn'): b differ, s1=None ≠ rn → REJECTED?! But this is a genuine alias case! Ugh.\n\nHmm. \"Globo FC\" (RN club) vs \"Globo - RN\" — the RN suffix should transfer. vs \"atletico nacional\" vs \"atletico-mg\" — must reject. Difference: in the Globo case, the suffixless base \"globo fc\" contains \"globo\"+something where the something is a LEGAL FORM (\"fc\"). In the nacional case \"atletico nacional\" contains \"atletico\" + \"nacional\" — \"nacional\" is not a legal form. I can whitelist trailing tokens that are club-legal forms: {\"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"esporte clube\", \"futebol\", \"fr\", \"pa\", \"sa\", \"ca\", \"ce\", \"ge\", \"se\", \"sp\", \"fbc\", \"ecb\"}? For the subset case with one suffixless: strip legal-form tokens from the longer base; if the REMAINING core equals the shorter base → accept, transferring the suffix. \"globo fc\" → strip \"fc\" → \"globo\" == \"globo\" ✓ accept → alias (\"globo fc\" → globo, rn). \"atletico nacional\" → strip \"nacional\"? not a legal form → \"atletico nacional\" != \"atletico\" → REJECT ✓. \n\nAnd for subset with BOTH suffixless or both same suffix: \"ae altos\" vs \"altos\"? — wait \"altos-pi\" vs \"ae altos\": b1='altos' s1='pi'; b2='ae altos' s2=None. Longer base \"ae altos\" — strip leading legal token \"ae\" → \"altos\" == shorter ✓ accept. Also strip LEADING legal tokens (\"ae\", \"ca\", \"ce\", \"sc\", \"ge\", \"se\", \"ad\", \"urt\"...). And TRAILING legal tokens (\"ec\", \"fc\", \"sc\", \"clube\"...). So normalize: strip leading/trailing legal-form tokens from the longer base, compare to shorter.\n\nCases from the list:\n- 'ae altos' → strip 'ae' → 'altos' ✓\n- 'ce dom bosco' → 'dom bosco' ✓ (vs 'dom bosco-mt')\n- 'sc genus' → 'genus' ✓\n- 'globo fc' → 'globo' ✓\n- 'amadense ec' → 'amadense' ✓\n- 'metropolitano maringa' vs 'maringa': 'metropolitano maringa' — 'metropolitano' is NOT a legal form... reject?? But it IS a genuine dup (Grêmio Metropolitano?). Hmm. Wait actually — hold on. Let me check the cup raw name for this. Maybe it's \"Metropolitano-PR\"? Let me grep. If cup raw = \"Metropolitano - PR\" → base \"metropolitano\" not \"metropolitano maringa\". The observed key was 'metropolitano maringa-pr' — hmm that means cup raw contains \"Metropolitano Maringá\". Hmm... hmm, that's odd. Let me check the actual raw.\n\nRather than agonize, let me check the raw names for a few of these, then finalize the rule + manually add stragglers as static aliases if the generic rule misses them.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05bd6936e0013VccilIRRimQdZ","sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","messageID":"msg_05bd3effb001rAx8hi24VDISj8"}}
