{"type":"text","timestamp":1788247358376,"sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","part":{"id":"prt_05bd8e29a001BbOLfbXD2x0Ewp","messageID":"msg_05bd69512001IGMi9Oc9Qlgztl","sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","type":"text","text":"I'll implement a data-driven alias-learning pass: after the first load, fixture evidence (same competition/season/date±1/score + identical opponent) reveals split team keys, which are learned as aliases and the data is re-loaded. This avoids hand-enumerating 57 small clubs while guarding against false merges (e.g. Atlético Nacional ≠ Atlético-MG).","time":{"start":1788247335578,"end":1788247358373}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788247358387,"sessionID":"ses_fa45f1af7ffepwbxxISA0FiJ2d","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_d749dae426fa411ba6916970","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-80ffd0e4ce9f/data_loader.py","newString":"    def _learn_aliases(self) -> dict[str, tuple[str, Optional[str]]]:\n        \"\"\"Detect team-key splits across sources and derive new aliases.\n\n        Two rows describing the same fixture (same competition, season,\n        score and date within one day) where one team key matches exactly\n        and the other differs prove that the two spellings are the same\n        club. The differing key of the later-loaded row is mapped onto the\n        key of the first-loaded row as a new BASE_ALIASES entry, provided\n        the names are compatible (same state suffix, or the suffixless\n        side reduces to the other's tokens after dropping legal-form\n        words like 'EC', 'FC', 'Clube'). Strict compatibility guards\n        against merging genuinely different clubs (e.g. Atletico Nacional\n        vs Atletico-MG).\n        \"\"\"\n        legal_tokens = {\n            \"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"ca\", \"ce\", \"ge\", \"se\", \"ae\",\n            \"ad\", \"de\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"futebol\", \"cr\", \"cs\",\n            \"sp\", \"pa\", \"urt\", \"gremio\", \"club atletico\",\n        }\n\n        def parts(key: str) -> tuple[str, Optional[str]]:\n            if \"-\" in key:\n                base, suffix = key.rsplit(\"-\", 1)\n                return base, suffix\n            return key, None\n\n        def compatible(k1: str, k2: str) -> bool:\n            b1, s1 = parts(k1)\n            b2, s2 = parts(k2)\n            if b1 == b2:\n                return k1 == k2 or s1 is None or s2 is None\n            if s1 and s2:\n                if s1 != s2:\n                    return False\n                return set(b1.split()) <= set(b2.split()) or set(b2.split()) <= set(b1.split())\n            if s2 is None and s1:\n                core = [t for t in b2.split() if t not in legal_tokens]\n                return set(core) <= set(b1.split())\n            if s1 is None and s2:\n                core = [t for t in b1.split() if t not in legal_tokens]\n                return set(core) <= set(b2.split())\n            return False\n\n        from normalize import BASE_ALIASES\n\n        by_fixture: dict[tuple, list[Match]] = defaultdict(list)\n        for match in self.matches:\n            if match.score and match.date is not None and match.season is not None:\n                for delta in (-1, 0, 1):\n                    ordinal = match.date.toordinal() + delta\n                    by_fixture[\n                        (\n                            match.competition,\n                            match.season,\n                            ordinal,\n                            match.score,\n                            match.away_key,\n                        )\n                    ].append(match)\n                    by_fixture[\n                        (\n                            match.competition,\n                            match.season,\n                            ordinal,\n                            match.score,\n                            (\"h\", match.home_key),\n                        )\n                    ].append(match)\n\n        learned: dict[str, tuple[str, Optional[str]]] = {}\n        seen_pairs: set[tuple] = set()\n        for bucket in by_fixture.values():\n            if len(bucket) < 2:\n                continue\n            for i, m1 in enumerate(bucket):\n                for m2 in bucket[i + 1:]:\n                    if m1 is m2:\n                        continue\n                    if self.matches.index(m1) > self.matches.index(m2):\n                        m1, m2 = m2, m1\n                    pairs = []\n                    if m1.home_key == m2.home_key and m1.away_key != m2.away_key:\n                        pairs = [(m2.away_key, m1.away_key)]\n                    elif m1.away_key == m2.away_key and m1.home_key != m2.home_key:\n                        pairs = [(m2.home_key, m1.home_key)]\n                    for bad_key, good_key in pairs:\n                        if not bad_key or not good_key or not compatible(good_key, bad_key):\n                            continue\n                        sig = (bad_key, good_key)\n                        if sig in seen_pairs:\n                            continue\n                        seen_pairs.add(sig)\n                        bad_base = bad_key.rsplit(\"-\", 1)[0]\n                        good_base, good_suffix = parts(good_key)\n                        if bad_base in BASE_ALIASES or bad_base in learned:\n                            continue\n                        learned[bad_base] = (good_base, good_suffix)\n        return learned\n\n    def _drop_misfiled_league_matches(self) -> None:","oldString":"    def _drop_misfiled_league_matches(self) -> None:"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-80ffd0e4ce9f/data_loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-80ffd0e4ce9f/data_loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-80ffd0e4ce9f/data_loader.py\n@@ -407,8 +407,107 @@\n             skills=skills,\n         )\n         self.players.append(player)\n \n+def _learn_aliases(self) -> dict[str, tuple[str, Optional[str]]]:\n+    \"\"\"Detect team-key splits across sources and derive new aliases.\n+\n+    Two rows describing the same fixture (same competition, season,\n+    score and date within one day) where one team key matches exactly\n+    and the other differs prove that the two spellings are the same\n+    club. The differing key of the later-loaded row is mapped onto the\n+    key of the first-loaded row as a new BASE_ALIASES entry, provided\n+    the names are compatible (same state suffix, or the suffixless\n+    side reduces to the other's tokens after dropping legal-form\n+    words like 'EC', 'FC', 'Clube'). Strict compatibility guards\n+    against merging genuinely different clubs (e.g. Atletico Nacional\n+    vs Atletico-MG).\n+    \"\"\"\n+    legal_tokens = {\n+        \"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"ca\", \"ce\", \"ge\", \"se\", \"ae\",\n+        \"ad\", \"de\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"futebol\", \"cr\", \"cs\",\n+        \"sp\", \"pa\", \"urt\", \"gremio\", \"club atletico\",\n+    }\n+\n+    def parts(key: str) -> tuple[str, Optional[str]]:\n+        if \"-\" in key:\n+            base, suffix = key.rsplit(\"-\", 1)\n+            return base, suffix\n+        return key, None\n+\n+    def compatible(k1: str, k2: str) -> bool:\n+        b1, s1 = parts(k1)\n+        b2, s2 = parts(k2)\n+        if b1 == b2:\n+            return k1 == k2 or s1 is None or s2 is None\n+        if s1 and s2:\n+            if s1 != s2:\n+                return False\n+            return set(b1.split()) <= set(b2.split()) or set(b2.split()) <= set(b1.split())\n+        if s2 is None and s1:\n+            core = [t for t in b2.split() if t not in legal_tokens]\n+            return set(core) <= set(b1.split())\n+        if s1 is None and s2:\n+            core = [t for t in b1.split() if t not in legal_tokens]\n+            return set(core) <= set(b2.split())\n+        return False\n+\n+    from normalize import BASE_ALIASES\n+\n+    by_fixture: dict[tuple, list[Match]] = defaultdict(list)\n+    for match in self.matches:\n+        if match.score and match.date is not None and match.season is not None:\n+            for delta in (-1, 0, 1):\n+                ordinal = match.date.toordinal() + delta\n+                by_fixture[\n+                    (\n+                        match.competition,\n+                        match.season,\n+                        ordinal,\n+                        match.score,\n+                        match.away_key,\n+                    )\n+                ].append(match)\n+                by_fixture[\n+                    (\n+                        match.competition,\n+                        match.season,\n+                        ordinal,\n+                        match.score,\n+                        (\"h\", match.home_key),\n+                    )\n+                ].append(match)\n+\n+    learned: dict[str, tuple[str, Optional[str]]] = {}\n+    seen_pairs: set[tuple] = set()\n+    for bucket in by_fixture.values():\n+        if len(bucket) < 2:\n+            continue\n+        for i, m1 in enumerate(bucket):\n+            for m2 in bucket[i + 1:]:\n+                if m1 is m2:\n+                    continue\n+                if self.matches.index(m1) > self.matches.index(m2):\n+                    m1, m2 = m2, m1\n+                pairs = []\n+                if m1.home_key == m2.home_key and m1.away_key != m2.away_key:\n+                    pairs = [(m2.away_key, m1.away_key)]\n+                elif m1.away_key == m2.away_key and m1.home_key != m2.home_key:\n+                    pairs = [(m2.home_key, m1.home_key)]\n+                for bad_key, good_key in pairs:\n+                    if not bad_key or not good_key or not compatible(good_key, bad_key):\n+                        continue\n+                    sig = (bad_key, good_key)\n+                    if sig in seen_pairs:\n+                        continue\n+                    seen_pairs.add(sig)\n+                    bad_base = bad_key.rsplit(\"-\", 1)[0]\n+                    good_base, good_suffix = parts(good_key)\n+                    if bad_base in BASE_ALIASES or bad_base in learned:\n+                        continue\n+                    learned[bad_base] = (good_base, good_suffix)\n+    return learned\n+\n def _drop_misfiled_league_matches(self) -> None:\n     \"\"\"Remove stray non-league rows mislabeled as league matches.\n \n     The extended-stats source occasionally tags state-league or cup\n","filediff":{"file":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-80ffd0e4ce9f/data_loader.py","patch":"Index: /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-80ffd0e4ce9f/data_loader.py\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-80ffd0e4ce9f/data_loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-80ffd0e4ce9f/data_loader.py\n@@ -407,8 +407,107 @@\n             skills=skills,\n         )\n         self.players.append(player)\n \n+def _learn_aliases(self) -> dict[str, tuple[str, Optional[str]]]:\n+    \"\"\"Detect team-key splits across sources and derive new aliases.\n+\n+    Two rows describing the same fixture (same competition, season,\n+    score and date within one day) where one team key matches exactly\n+    and the other differs prove that the two spellings are the same\n+    club. The differing key of the later-loaded row is mapped onto the\n+    key of the first-loaded row as a new BASE_ALIASES entry, provided\n+    the names are compatible (same state suffix, or the suffixless\n+    side reduces to the other's tokens after dropping legal-form\n+    words like 'EC', 'FC', 'Clube'). Strict compatibility guards\n+    against merging genuinely different clubs (e.g. Atletico Nacional\n+    vs Atletico-MG).\n+    \"\"\"\n+    legal_tokens = {\n+        \"fc\", \"ec\", \"sc\", \"ac\", \"aa\", \"ca\", \"ce\", \"ge\", \"se\", \"ae\",\n+        \"ad\", \"de\", \"clube\", \"club\", \"esporte\", \"futebol\", \"cr\", \"cs\",\n+        \"sp\", \"pa\", \"urt\", \"gremio\", \"club atletico\",\n+    }\n+\n+    def parts(key: str) -> tuple[str, Optional[str]]:\n+        if \"-\" in key:\n+            base, suffix = key.rsplit(\"-\", 1)\n+            return base, suffix\n+        return key, None\n+\n+    def compatible(k1: str, k2: str) -> bool:\n+        b1, s1 = parts(k1)\n+        b2, s2 = parts(k2)\n+        if b1 == b2:\n+            return k1 == k2 or s1 is None or s2 is None\n+        if s1 and s2:\n+            if s1 != s2:\n+                return False\n+            return set(b1.split()) <= set(b2.split()) or set(b2.split()) <= set(b1.split())\n+        if s2 is None and s1:\n+            core = [t for t in b2.split() if t not in legal_tokens]\n+            return set(core) <= set(b1.split())\n+        if s1 is None and s2:\n+            core = [t for t in b1.split() if t not in legal_tokens]\n+            return set(core) <= set(b2.split())\n+        return False\n+\n+    from normalize import BASE_ALIASES\n+\n+    by_fixture: dict[tuple, list[Match]] = defaultdict(list)\n+    for match in self.matches:\n+        if match.score and match.date is not None and match.season is not None:\n+            for delta in (-1, 0, 1):\n+                ordinal = match.date.toordinal() + delta\n+                by_fixture[\n+                    (\n+                        match.competition,\n+                        match.season,\n+                        ordinal,\n+                        match.score,\n+                        match.away_key,\n+                    )\n+                ].append(match)\n+                by_fixture[\n+                    (\n+                        match.competition,\n+                        match.season,\n+                        ordinal,\n+                        match.score,\n+                        (\"h\", match.home_key),\n+                    )\n+                ].append(match)\n+\n+    learned: dict[str, tuple[str, Optional[str]]] = {}\n+    seen_pairs: set[tuple] = set()\n+    for bucket in by_fixture.values():\n+        if len(bucket) < 2:\n+            continue\n+        for i, m1 in enumerate(bucket):\n+            for m2 in bucket[i + 1:]:\n+                if m1 is m2:\n+                    continue\n+                if self.matches.index(m1) > self.matches.index(m2):\n+                    m1, m2 = m2, m1\n+                pairs = []\n+                if m1.home_key == m2.home_key and m1.away_key != m2.away_key:\n+                    pairs = [(m2.away_key, m1.away_key)]\n+                elif m1.away_key == m2.away_key and m1.home_key != m2.home_key:\n+                    pairs = [(m2.home_key, m1.home_key)]\n+                for bad_key, good_key in pairs:\n+                    if not bad_key or not good_key or not compatible(good_key, bad_key):\n+                        continue\n+                    sig = (bad_key, good_key)\n+                    if sig in seen_pairs:\n+                        continue\n+                    seen_pairs.add(sig)\n+                    bad_base = bad_key.rsplit(\"-\", 1)[0]\n+                    good_base, good_suffix = parts(good_key)\n+                    if bad_base in BASE_ALIASES or bad_base in learned:\n+                        continue\n+                    learned[bad_base] = (good_base, good_suffix)\n+    return learned\n+\n def _drop_misfiled_league_matches(self) -> None:\n     \"\"\"Remove stray non-league rows mislabeled as league matches.\n \n     The extended-stats source occasionally tags state-league or cup\n","additions":99,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"data_loader.py","time":{"start":1788247358382,"end":1788247358384}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Very interesting — the detector output earlier was inverted from what I assumed! E.g. for the 2015-03-05 Madureira match: it showed ('maringa-pr', 'metropolitano maringa-pr', 'madureira-rj') — \"Metropolitano Maringa PR\" is BR-FOOTBALL (not cup!) — BR-Football names it \"Metropolitano Maringa PR\" (Grêmio Metropolitano... actually the club \"Grêmio Metropolitano Maringá\" hmm, or maybe Cianorte... whatever). And 'maringa-pr'... which source? \"Maringa\" is a BR-Football name too! So BOTH from BR-Football: \"Maringa\" and \"Metropolitano Maringa PR\" are two names for the same club within BR-Football?? OR — wait. Hmm, no: the pair (m1, m2) = both Copa do Brasil matches: one keyed 'maringa-pr' — from \"Maringa\" raw (BR-Football Copa do Brasil rows), other 'metropolitano maringa-pr' from \"Metropolitano Maringa PR\" (also BR-Football Copa rows?). Hmm — but wait, that can't both be BR-Football... Let me not over-analyze; the club is \"Grêmio Metropolitano Maringá\"... Hmm, actually no! I recall now: \"Maringá Futebol Clube\" vs \"Grêmio Metropolitano\"... no. Whatever — same fixture, same opponent, same date ±1, same score: it's the same match recorded under two names.\n\nAlso \"Retro\" vs \"Retro FC Brasil\" (both BR-Football): Retro FC Brasil = Retrô (PE club). \"Retro\" alone in BR-Football — same club. Pair: ('corinthians-sp', 'retro-pe', 'retro fc brasil')?? wait output was ('2021-03-26', 'Copa do Brasil', 'corinthians-sp', 'retro-pe', 'retro fc brasil') — so m1 home=corinthians-sp away=retro-pe; m2 away=retro fc brasil. 'retro-pe' — from CUP file \"Retrô - PE\"! And 'retro fc brasil' from BR-Football \"Retro FC Brasil\". Alias: \"retro fc brasil\" → (\"retro\",\"pe\") ✓ subset case with one suffixless: longer base \"retro fc brasil\" — strip legal \"fc\"... \"retro fc brasil\" → tokens: retro, fc, brasil — strip trailing \"brasil\"?? \"brasil\" is not a legal form... hmm! \"retro fc brasil\" → core after stripping fc = \"retro brasil\" ≠ \"retro\". FAILS my rule.\n\nOK — the token-legal-form rule is too narrow. New approach for the subset case: require that the shorter base appears as a TOKEN PREFIX or the longer base CONTAINS the shorter as a token sequence AND at least one of {suffix equal, shorter base equals longer base minus one legal token}. Ugh, complexity.\n\nSTEP BACK. Alternative simpler & safer: learn aliases from the fixture-pair evidence with a simple similarity: bases where ONE equals the other after removing legal-form tokens (leading/trailing) — that covers ae altos, ce dom bosco, sc genus, globo fc, amadense ec, 4 de julho ec, sinop fc, toledo ec, vilhenense ec, nova mutum ec, jaragua ec, floresta ec, cordino ec, anapolis fc, cs sergipe, operario fc (→ 'operario fc ms' has suffix ms... base 'operario fc' vs 'operario': strip 'fc' ✓), campinense clube ('clube' legal), boavista sc saquarema ('sc' legal but 'saquarema' isn't...) hmm — 'boavista sc saquarema' vs 'boavista': strip 'sc' → 'boavista saquarema' ≠ 'boavista'. FAILS.\n\nHmm. OK, different tactic — COMBINED evidence: for the \"one side identical\" fixture pairs, I have HIGH confidence they're the same match (same comp, same season, same date ±1, same score, opponent identical). The remaining question is only: which key is canonical? For learning, I don't need a clever name-similarity rule at all — the FIXTURE EVIDENCE ITSELF is the proof, provided the two differing keys are \"reasonably\" related (to avoid merging e.g. \"santos-sp\" vs \"santos laguna\" — which could theoretically host the same team... in the same competition+season+date±1+score? Santos (BRA) and Santos Laguna (MEX) in the same Libertadores season — they can't both host the same opponent on the same day with the same score... it's a real fixture collision risk only in theory).\n\nPragmatic decision: use fixture evidence + loose similarity: bases share a common token ≥4 chars (e.g. \"maringa\" shared; \"boavista\" shared; \"retro\" shared; \"globo\" shared; \"operario\" shared; \"pelotas\" hmm — 'brasil-rs' vs 'brasil de pelotas-rs' shares \"brasil\" ✓; \"serra\" ✓; \"altos\" ✓; \"campinense\" ✓; \"sergipe\" ✓... wait 'cs sergipe' vs 'sergipe-se' shares \"sergipe\" ✓). BUT this would also merge \"sport\" vs \"sport boys\" (share \"sport\", 5 chars!) — but as computed, that pairing requires same opponent+date+score+season — Sport and Sport Boys never both host the same team in the same Libertadores season on the same date. It's safe ENOUGH given fixture evidence. And \"atletico nacional\" vs \"atletico-mg\": share \"atletico\" (7 chars) — same-season same-competition both hosting the SAME opponent with the same score on the same day? Atlético Nacional and Atlético-MG both played in Libertadores 2014, 2016, 2017, 2019... could both host e.g. \"Independiente\" hmm. Same DATE ±1 with SAME SCORE — the fixtures would have to collide. Extremely unlikely; and even if it merged 2 matches, the impact is 2 rows. But wait — \"atletico nacional\" vs \"atletico-mg\": bases \"atletico nacional\" vs \"atletico\" — subset AND share token. Under my rule → LEARN alias \"atletico nacional\" → (\"atletico\",\"mg\") — this would then apply to ALL Atlético Nacional matches, remapping the Colombian club into Atlético Mineiro!!! That's catastrophic (not just 2 rows — every Nacional match would merge into Mineiro standings!). NO — the alias applies to base \"atletico nacional\"; all \"Atlético Nacional\" matches → \"atletico-mg\" — then h2h Libertadores junk. MUST NOT HAPPEN.\n\nOK so the loose-similarity is dangerous for ALIAS LEARNING even if fixture evidence is rare, because the learned alias applies globally. Safety rule: only learn when suffixes agree when both present; and when one is suffixless, require the suffixless base to be \"explained\" by the other: suffixless_base == longer_base OR suffixless_base's token-subset after stripping legal-form tokens matches. For \"metropolitano maringa pr\" vs \"maringa\"... wait 'metropolitano maringa-pr' HAS suffix pr; 'maringa-pr' has suffix pr — EQUAL suffixes ✓. Bases \"metropolitano maringa\" vs \"maringa\" differ but subset + equal suffixes → learn \"maringa\" → (\"metropolitano maringa\",\"pr\")?? Hmm wait which is canonical (kept)? Load order: cup file loads 3rd, BR-Football 5th. The 'maringa-pr' match — hmm both are BR-Football names! \"Maringa\" and \"Metropolitano Maringa PR\" both in BR-Football... TWO matches within the same file?? The cup file has no Maringá-Metropolitano. So the duplicate pair is WITHIN BR-Football: their Copa do Brasil rows name the club inconsistently (\"Maringa\" in the fixture row vs \"Metropolitano Maringa PR\" in another row?). That seems weird... OR the 'maringa-pr' one came from CUP file via a different raw name? Cup names: no 'Maringa' in cup (checked: cup has no Metropolitano/Maringá entries printed — wait the grep showed cup: [] for Metropolitano but didn't grep Maringa in cup). Let me grep 'Maring' in cup.\n\nUgh, this rabbit hole is deep. DECISION: implement the alias-learning pass with STRICT rules, then PRINT the learned aliases and inspect them once. Rules:\n- Fixture evidence: same competition, same season, date ±1, same score, one side key IDENTICAL, other side keys DIFFERENT.\n- The differing keys must pass: (suffixes equal) OR (one suffixless and the suffixless base ⊆ suffixed base's tokens, or bases equal).\n  - Actually simplify: suffixes must be EQUAL, or one is None and its base is a token-subset of the other base.\n  - Token-subset: every token of shorter base appears in longer base's tokens.\n  - \"globo fc\" (None) vs \"globo-rn\": tokens {globo, fc} ⊆ {globo}? NO — fc not in {globo}. FAILS. Hmm!! But it's a genuine alias...\n\nGah. OK let me handle the suffixless side: allow suffixless base to have EXTRA tokens beyond the suffixed base if those extra tokens are legal forms (fc/ec/sc/ae/ce/ca/ge/se/de/ad/clube/club/futebol/fbpa/spa/fria...). So: core(shorter_suffixless) after removing legal-form tokens must be ⊆ tokens of other base.\n- \"globo fc\": remove 'fc' → {globo} ⊆ {globo} ✓\n- \"ae altos\": remove 'ae' → {altos} ⊆ {altos} ✓\n- \"retro fc brasil\": remove 'fc' → {retro, brasil} ⊆ {retro}? NO — fails (genuinely needs manual alias)\n- \"atletico nacional\" (suffixless) vs \"atletico-mg\": remove legal forms → {atletico, nacional} ⊆ {atletico}? NO ✓ correctly rejected\n- \"brasil de pelotas-rs\" vs \"brasil-rs\": BOTH have suffix rs → equal suffixes → accept, bases differ, subset {brasil} ⊆ {brasil, de, pelotas} ✓ learn \"brasil de pelotas\" → hmm which direction? Canonical = kept match's key. Kept = earlier in load = cup file's \"brasil-rs\"?? WAIT — is \"Brasil - RS\" (cup) loading before BR-Football \"Brasil de Pelotas\"? Yes (cup 3rd, brfoot 5th). So learned: bad=\"brasil de pelotas\" → good=(\"brasil\",\"rs\"). CONFLICT with my static \"brasil de pelotas\" → (\"brasil de pelotas\",\"rs\")! The static produces a different key → static wins → dup remains. FIX: remove the static \"brasil de pelotas\" alias. And add DISPLAY_NAMES[\"brasil-rs\"] = \"Brasil de Pelotas\". ✓\n- \"maringa\" static → (\"maringa\",\"pr\") vs learned would be... whatever; check output.\n\nAlso for equal-suffix subset case: is \"santos laguna\" (None suffix) vs \"santos-sp\"? suffixes None vs sp — one suffixless: core {santos, laguna} ⊆ {santos}? NO ✓ rejected.\n- \"santos-sp\" vs \"santos fc\"? static has \"santos fc\" → (\"santos\",\"sp\") ✓ already unified.\n\nThe remaining manual stragglers (\"retro fc brasil\", \"boavista sc saquarema\", \"metropolitano maringa\"?, \"operario ferroviario esporte c\"...) — I'll add static aliases after seeing what the learner finds. Let me now implement:\n\nIn data_loader, after first full load:\n```python\ndef _learn_aliases(self) -> dict[str, tuple[str, Optional[str]]]:\n    # index: (comp, season, ordinal, score, away_key) -> [matches] for home-side compare\n    from normalize import BASE_ALIASES\n    learned = {}\n    def base_suffix(key):\n        if '-' in key:\n            b, s = key.rsplit('-', 1)\n            return b, s\n        return key, None\n    LEGAL = {'fc','ec','sc','ac','aa','ca','ce','ge','se','ae','ad','de','urt','clube','club','esporte','futebol','sp','pa','cr','cs'}\n    def compatible(k1, k2):\n        b1, s1 = base_suffix(k1); b2, s2 = base_suffix(k2)\n        if b1 == b2 and k1 != k2:\n            return s1 is None or s2 is None\n        if s1 and s2 and s1 != s2: return False\n        if s1 and s2 and s1 == s2:\n            t1, t2 = set(b1.split()), set(b2.split())\n            return t1 <= t2 or t2 <= t1\n        # one suffixless\n        if s2 is None:  # k1 has suffix, k2 not\n            core = [t for t in b2.split() if t not in LEGAL]\n            return set(core) <= set(b1.split())\n        core = [t for t in b1.split() if t not in LEGAL]\n        return set(core) <= set(b2.split())\n    ...\n```\nThen fixture matching: for each pair (m1, m2) with m1.idx < m2.idx (m1 kept), same comp+season+score, date±1, and (home equal & away differ & compatible) → learn bad=away2 → good=(base1,suf1); or (away equal & home differ & compatible) → learn bad=home2 → good.\n\nLearn: learned[base_of(bad_key)] = (base_of(good_key), suffix_of(good_key)) — only if base_of(bad) not already in BASE_ALIASES (or is in BASE_ALIASES but produces different result? no — skip existing).\n\nHmm wait — careful: \"bad base\" might equal \"good base\" (e.g. 'novo hamburgo' vs 'novo hamburgo-rs' — bad base \"novo hamburgo\", good (\"novo hamburgo\",\"rs\")). BASE_ALIASES[\"novo hamburgo\"] = (\"novo hamburgo\",\"rs\") — that's fine (identity base + uf).\n\nThen `get_data()`:\n```python\ndef get_data(dir=DATA_DIR):\n    global _INSTANCE\n    if _INSTANCE is None:\n        with _LOCK:\n            if _INSTANCE is None:\n                first = SoccerData(dir)\n                aliases = first._learn_aliases()\n                if aliases:\n                    normalize.BASE_ALIASES.update(aliases)\n                    _INSTANCE = SoccerData(dir)   # reload with learned aliases\n                else:\n                    _INSTANCE = first\n    return _INSTANCE\n```\nImport BASE_ALIASES from normalize for update — need `from normalize import BASE_ALIASES` then BASE_ALIASES.update(...). (Module-level dict mutation ✓.)\n\nBut careful: learned aliases keyed by base; a base might collide with an existing alias → skip those (existing statics are deliberate). Also potential learned-learning conflicts (two fixtures teach different mappings for same base) — keep first, log others? Just keep first