er pairs. I need an additional bucket dimension: for the both-sides-differ case, bucket by... something looser. E.g., bucket by (comp, season, ordinal, score) alone and compare within — but those buckets are big (10+ matches per day per comp with same score — pair explosion but manageable: total pairs across buckets ~ sum(n²) — with 17k matches and typical bucket sizes small... a (comp, season, date, score) bucket could hold ~5-15 matches; pairs ~100 per bucket × #buckets(~17k×3) — up to millions of comparisons, each cheap. Actually fine in Python? 17k matches × 3 ordinals = 51k entries across maybe 15k distinct buckets, average size 3-4 → pairs ~ 51k×4 = 200k comparisons ✓ fast.\n\nAdd third bucket: key (comp, season, ordinal, score) → matches. In the pair loop for these buckets, the single-side-equal cases duplicate what the away/home buckets do (seen_pairs dedups), and both-sides-differ + both-compatible → learn. Let me restructure: keep existing buckets, add the generic bucket. To avoid double-learning conflicts, seen_pairs guards.\n\n2. `('afogados da ingazeira fc', 'afogados-pe')` — both suffixless vs pe? 'afogados da ingazeira fc' s=None, 'afogados-pe' s='pe'. core of suffixless: 'fc' legal → {afogados, da→legal, ingazeira} ⊆ {afogados}? NO → reject. Genuine alias though (Afogados da Ingazeira-PE). Static: \"afogados da ingazeira fc\" → (\"afogados\",\"pe\").\n\n3. `('esportivo bento goncalves', 'esportivo-rs')` — core {esportivo?? wait 'esportivo' not legal → {esportivo, bento, goncalves} ⊆ {esportivo}? NO → reject. Static: \"esportivo bento goncalves\" → (\"esportivo\",\"rs\")? Hmm — \"Esportivo-RS\" (Bento Gonçalves club) ✓ static.\n\n4. `('ceo varzeagrandense', 'operario-mt')` — CEO Varzeagrandense vs Operário-MT?? Different club NAMES entirely (Varzeagrandense is from Várzea Grande-MT; Operário-MT... hmm wait — \"Operário Futebol Clube (Várzea Grande)\"? Hmm — hmm! There's \"CEO Varzeagrandense\" (CEO = Clube Esportivo Operário? Várzea Grande-MT). YES: CEO = \"Clube Esportivo Operário\" of Várzea Grande — the SAME club as \"Operário-MT\"! Fixture evidence: same date/score/opponent ✓. Names totally different — learner can't. Static: \"ceo varzeagrandense\" → (\"operario\",\"mt\")? Hmm wait — which is canonical: cup has \"Operário - MT\" → 'operario-mt'; brfoot \"CEO Varzeagrandense\" → static \"ceo varzeagrandense\" → (\"operario\",\"mt\") ✓.\n\n5. `('uniao de rondonopolis-mt', 'uniao-mt')` — bases 'uniao de rondonopolis' vs 'uniao', suffixes mt==mt equal → token subset {uniao} ⊆ {uniao, de, rondonopolis} ✓ compatible → should learn 'uniao' → ('uniao de rondonopolis','mt'). Why not learned? Because... bad_base 'uniao' — wait 'uniao' base in BASE_ALIASES? NO. Hmm — but wait, the LEARNED direction: kept = earlier = 'uniao de rondonopolis-mt' (cup, 3rd) — bad='uniao-mt' (brfoot, 5th) → learn 'uniao' → ('uniao de rondonopolis','mt') ✓ should work via single-side bucket (away equal). Let me check the fixture: 2020-02-06 both... earlier print showed 'uniao-mt vs atletico-go' (brfoot) — and cup 'uniao de rondonopolis-mt' vs atletico-go? away keys equal ✓ single-side bucket SHOULD catch. Hmm!! Unless 'uniao' IS in learned from a conflicting earlier fixture and got skipped (learned dict first-wins). E.g. 'uniao' learned from another pair earlier in iteration → ('uniao', something else) — order matters. Whatever — the result: 'uniao-mt' vs 'uniao de rondonopolis-mt' still separate. Hmm wait — actually, let me check what learned has for 'uniao' after this run.\n\nActually — let me stop iterating blindly. The proper fix: add the (comp, season, ordinal, score) generic bucket to the learner (fixes both-sides-differ AND lets pairs be found regardless of bucket side), add the 4 statics, re-run, and check what remains. Remaining residuals will be: genuine different-club coincidences (cruzeiro/sport 2008, river-ac/pi, central-pe/sc, sao jose-pa/rs, crb/parana, goias/sampaio, bragantino-pa/vila nova?? wait bragantino-pa vs vila nova-g